[856.751029, "o", " |      Returns\r\n |      -------\r\n |      List of str\r\n |          list of all roles defined in the graph.\r\n |\r\n |  h\bha\bas\bs_\b_r\bro\bol\ble\be(self, role: str) -> bool\r\n |      Check if a role is defined and non-empty.\r\n |\r\n |      Parameters\r\n |      ----------\r\n |      role : str\r\n |          The name of the role to check.\r\n |\r\n |      Returns\r\n |      -------\r\n |      bool\r\n |          True if the role exists and has variables assigned, False otherwise."]
[856.751216, "o", "\r\n"]
[856.751386, "o", " |\r\n |  i\bis\bs_\b_v\bva\bal\bli\bid\bd_\b_c\bca\bau\bus\bsa\bal\bl_\b_s\bst\btr\bru\buc\bct\btu\bur\bre\be(self) -> bool\r\n |      Validate that the causal structure makes sense.\r\n |\r\n |  w\bwi\bit\bth\bh_\b_r\bro\bol\ble\be(self, role: str, variables, inplace=False)\r\n |      Return a new graph with the specified role assignment.\r\n |\r\n |      Parameters\r\n |      ----------\r\n |      role : str\r\n"]
[856.751639, "o", " |          The name of the role to assign, e.g., \"exposures\", \"outcomes\".\r\n |      variables : str, set, list, or any iterable\r\n |          The variables to assign to the role.\r\n |      inplace=False : bool, optional\r\n |          If True, modifies the current graph in place. Defaults to False.\r\n |\r\n |      Returns\r\n |      -------\r\n |      graph of same type as self\r\n |          A new instance with the specified role assigned, to the variables provided.\r\n |\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m"]
[856.751715, "o", "\r |  w\bwi\bit\bth\bho\bou\but\bt_\b_r\bro\bol\ble\be(self, role: str, variables=None, inplace=False)\r\n |      Return a new graph with the specified role removed.\r\n |\r\n |      Parameters\r\n |      ----------\r\n |      role : str\r\n |          The name of the role to remove, e.g., \"exposures\", \"outcomes\".\r\n |      variables : str, set, list, or iterable, default = all variables with the role\r\n |          The variables to remove the role from. If not provided,\r\n |          all variables with the specified role will have it removed.\r\n"]
[856.752018, "o", " |      inplace=False : bool, optional\r\n |          If True, modifies the current graph in place. Defaults to False.\r\n |\r\n |      Returns\r\n |      -------\r\n |      graph of same type as self\r\n |          A new instance with the specified role removed from all nodes that had it.\r\n |\r\n |  ----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n |  Readonly properties inherited from pgmpy.base._mixin_roles._GraphRolesMixin:\r\n |\r\n |  o\bob\bbs\bse\ber\brv\bve\bed\bd\r\n |      Returns the set of observed variables in the causal model.\r\n |\r\n |      Property\r\n"]
[856.752096, "o", " |      --------\r\n |      observed: set of nodes (default: empty set)\r\n |          A set of observed variables in the graph. These are the variables\r\n |          that can be measured directed and have data available for them.\r\n |\r\n |      Examples\r\n |      --------\r\n |      Create a DAG with latents and check the observed value.\r\n |\r\n |      >>> from pgmpy.base import DAG\r\n"]
[856.75241, "o", " |      >>> G = DAG(ebunch=[(\"a\", \"b\")], latents=\"a\")\r\n |      >>> G.observed\r\n |      {'b'}\r\n |\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m\r |  ----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m"]
[895.288927, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > run_final.py\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom pgmpy.models import LinearGaussianBayesianNetwork\nfrom pgmpy.factors.continuous import LinearGaussianCPD\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/bn_sample_10k.csv')\n\nedges_parent_child = [\n    ('U', 'M'),\n    ('U', 'Y'),\n    ('U', 'D'),\n    ('Y', 'D'),\n    ('U', 'R'),\n    ('R', 'M')\n]\n\n# 1) Save learned_dag.csv\ndf_learned = pd.DataFrame([(child, parent) for parent, child in edges_parent_child], columns=['to', 'from'])\ndf_learned.to_csv('/app/learned_dag.csv', index=False)\nprint(\"Saved /app/learned_dag.csv\")\n\n# 2) Learn Bayesian Network\nmodel = LinearGaussianBayesianNetwork(edges_parent_child)\nmodel.fit(df)\n\n# 3) Perform causal intervention on Y: remove edges into Y ('U', 'Y')\nintervened_edges_pc = [e for e in edges_parent_child if e[1] != 'Y']\n\n# 4) Save intervened_dag.csv\ndf_intervened = pd.DataFrame([(child, parent) for parent, child in intervened_edges_pc], columns=['to', 'from'])\ndf_intervened.to_csv('/app/intervened_dag.csv', index=False)\nprint(\"Saved /app/intervened_dag.csv\")\n\n# Create intervened model and fit\nintervened_model = LinearGaussianBayesianNetwork(intervened_edges_pc)\nintervened_model.fit(df)\n\n# Override CPD of Y to be deterministic / zero variance around 0.0 (std = 1e-4, no parents)\ncpd_y = LinearGaussianCPD('Y', beta=[0.0], std=1e-4, evidence=[])\nintervened_model.add_cpds(cpd_y)\n\n# Get CPDs and topological ordering\ncpds = {cpd.variable: cpd for cpd in intervened_model.get_cpds()}\ntop_order = list(intervened_model.topological_sort())\nprint(\"Topological order:\", top_order)\n\n# Ancestral sampling 10k points\nn_samples = 10000\nsamples_dict = {}\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\nfor node in top_order:\n    cpd = cpds[node]\n    # cpd.beta: [intercept, coef1, coef2, ...]\n    beta = cpd.beta\n    std = cpd.std\n    evidence = cpd.evidence\n    \n    if not evidence:\n        mean = beta[0]\n    else:\n        mean = beta[0]\n        for i, parent in enumerate(evidence):\n            mean += beta[i+1] * samples_dict[parent]\n            \n    samples_dict[node] = np.random.normal(mean, std, size=n_samples)\n\nsamples_df = pd.DataFrame(samples_dict)\nsamples_df = samples_df[df.columns]\nsamples_df.to_csv('/app/final_bn_sample.csv', index=False)\nprint(\"Saved /app/final_bn_sample.csv with shape:\", samples_df.shape)\nprint(samples_df.head())\nEOF\n./venv/bin/python run_final.py\n"]
[895.289252, "o", "\u0007\r |  Data descriptors inherited from pgmpy.base._mixin_roles._GraphRolesMixin:\r\n |\r\n |  _\b__\b_d\bdi\bic\bct\bt_\b__\b_\r\n |      dictionary for instance variables\r\n |\r\n |  _\b__\b_w\bwe\bea\bak\bkr\bre\bef\bf_\b__\b_"]
[895.289332, "o", "\r\n |      list of weak references to the object\r\n |\r\n |  e\bex\bxp\bpo\bos\bsu\bur\bre\bes\bs\r\n |      Returns the set of exposure variables in the causal model.\r\n |\r\n |      Property\r\n |      --------\r\n |      exposures : set of nodes (default: empty set)\r\n"]
[895.289386, "o", " |          A set of exposure variables in the graph. These are the variables\r\n"]
[895.289628, "o", " |          that represent the treatment or intervention being studied in a\r\n |          causal analysis.\r\n |\r\n |      Examples\r\n |      --------\r\n |      Create a DAG with exposures and check the exposures value.\r\n |\r\n |      >>> from pgmpy.base import DAG\r\n |      >>> G = DAG(ebunch=[(\"a\", \"b\")], exposures=\"a\")\r\n |      >>> G.exposures\r\n |      {'a'}\r\n |\r\n |  l\bla\bat\bte\ben\bnt\bts\bs\r\n |      Returns the set of latent variables in the causal model.\r\n |\r\n |      Property\r\n |      --------\r\n |      latents : set of nodes (default: empty set)\r\n |          A set of latent variables in the graph. These are not observed\r\n"]
[895.28978, "o", " |          variables but are used to represent unobserved confounding or\r\n |          other latent structures.\r\n |\r\n |      Examples\r\n |      --------\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m\r\u001b[K\u001b[7mNo previous regular expression\u001b[27m\r |      Create a DAG with latents and check the latents value.\r\n\r |\r\n"]
[895.29001, "o", " |      >>> from pgmpy.base import DAG\r\n |      >>> G = DAG(ebunch=[(\"a\", \"b\")], latents=\"a\")\r\n |      >>> G.latents\r\n |      {'a'}\r\n |\r\n |  o\bou\but\btc\bco\bom\bme\bes\bs\r\n |      Returns the set of outcome variables in the causal model.\r\n |\r\n |      Property\r\n |      --------\r\n |      outcomes : set of nodes (default: empty set)\r\n |          A set of outcome variables in the graph. These are the variables\r\n"]
[895.290152, "o", " |          that represent the response or dependent variables being studied\r\n |          in a causal analysis.\r\n |\r\n |      Examples\r\n |      --------\r\n |      Create a DAG with outcomes and check the outcomes value.\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m\r |\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m\r\u001b[K\u001b[7mNo previous regular expression\u001b[27m\r |      >>> from pgmpy.base import DAG\r\n"]
[895.290252, "o", " |      >>> G = DAG(ebunch=[(\"a\", \"b\")], outcomes=\"b\")\r\n |      >>> G.outcomes\r\n |      {'b'}\r\n |\r\n |  ----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n |  Methods inherited from networkx.classes.digraph.DiGraph:\r\n |\r\n"]
[895.290302, "o", " |  a\bad\bdd\bd_\b_n\bno\bod\bde\be(self, node_for_adding, **attr)\r\n |      Add a single node `node_for_adding` and update node attributes.\r\n |\r\n"]
[895.290392, "o", " |      Parameters\r\n |      ----------\r\n |      node_for_adding : node\r\n |          A node can be any hashable Python object except None.\r\n"]
[895.290779, "o", " |      attr : keyword arguments, optional\r\n |          Set or change node attributes using key=value.\r\n |\r\n |      See Also\r\n |      --------\r\n |      add_nodes_from\r\n |\r\n |      Examples\r\n |      --------\r\n |      >>> G = nx.Graph()  # or DiGraph, MultiGraph, MultiDiGraph, etc\r\n |      >>> G.add_node(1)\r\n |      >>> G.add_node(\"Hello\")\r\n |      >>> K3 = nx.Graph([(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 0)])\r\n |      >>> G.add_node(K3)\r\n |      >>> G.number_of_nodes()\r\n |      3\r\n |\r\n |      Use keywords set/change node attributes:\r\n |\r\n |      >>> G.add_node(1, size=10)\r\n |      >>> G.add_node(3, weight=0.4, UTM=(\"13S\", 382871, 3972649))\r\n |\r\n |      Notes\r\n |      -----\r\n\r |      A hashable object is one that can be used as a key in a Python\r\n |      dictionary. This includes strings, numbers, tuples of strings\r\n |      and numbers, etc.\r\n |\r\n |      On many platforms hashable items also include mutables such as\r\n |      NetworkX Graphs, though one should be careful that the hash\r\n |      doesn't change on mutables.\r\n |\r\n |  a\bad\bdd\bd_\b_n\bno\bod\bde\bes\bs_\b_f\bfr\bro\bom\bm(self, nodes_for_adding, **attr)\r\n |      Add multiple nodes.\r\n |\r\n |      Parameters\r\n |      ----------\r\n |      nodes_for_adding : iterable container\r\n |          A container of nodes (list, dict, set, etc.).\r\n |          OR\r\n |          A container of (node, attribute dict) tuples.\r\n |          Node attributes are updated using the attribute dict.\r\n"]
[895.290909, "o", " |      attr : keyword arguments, optional (default= no attributes)\r\n |          Update attributes for all nodes in nodes.\r\n |          Node attributes specified in nodes as a tuple take\r\n |          precedence over attributes specified via keyword arguments.\r\n |\r\n |      See Also\r\n"]
[895.291098, "o", " |      --------\r\n |      add_node\r\n |\r\n |      Notes\r\n |      -----\r\n |      When adding nodes from an iterator over the graph you are changing,\r\n"]
[895.291231, "o", " |      a `RuntimeError` can be raised with message:\r\n |      `RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration`. This\r\n |      happens when the graph's underlying dictionary is modified during\r\n |      iteration. To avoid this error, evaluate the iterator into a separate\r\n |      object, e.g. by using `list(iterator_of_nodes)`, and pass this\r\n |      object to `G.add_nodes_from`.\r\n |\r\n |      Examples\r\n |      --------\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m"]
[895.291381, "o", "\r |      >>> G = nx.Graph()  # or DiGraph, MultiGraph, MultiDiGraph, etc\r\n |      >>> G.add_nodes_from(\"Hello\")\r\n |      >>> K3 = nx.Graph([(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 0)])\r\n"]
[895.291482, "o", " |      >>> G.add_nodes_from(K3)\r\n |      >>> sorted(G.nodes(), key=str)\r\n |      [0, 1, 2, 'H', 'e', 'l', 'o']\r\n |\r\n |      Use keywords to update specific node attributes for every node.\r\n |\r\n"]
[895.291711, "o", " |      >>> G.add_nodes_from([1, 2], size=10)\r\n |      >>> G.add_nodes_from([3, 4], weight=0.4)\r\n |\r\n |      Use (node, attrdict) tuples to update attributes for specific nodes.\r\n |\r\n |      >>> G.add_nodes_from([(1, dict(size=11)), (2, {\"color\": \"blue\"})])\r\n"]
[895.291908, "o", " |      >>> G.nodes[1][\"size\"]\r\n |      11\r\n |      >>> H = nx.Graph()\r\n |      >>> H.add_nodes_from(G.nodes(data=True))\r\n |      >>> H.nodes[1][\"size\"]\r\n |      11\r\n |\r\n |      Evaluate an iterator over a graph if using it to modify the same graph\r\n |\r\n |      >>> G = nx.DiGraph([(0, 1), (1, 2), (3, 4)])\r\n |      >>> # wrong way - will raise RuntimeError\r\n |      >>> # G.add_nodes_from(n + 1 for n in G.nodes)\r\n |      >>> # correct way\r\n"]
[895.292005, "o", " |      >>> G.add_nodes_from(list(n + 1 for n in G.nodes))\r\n |\r\n |  a\bad\bdj\bj = <functools.cached_property object>\r\n |      Graph adjacency object holding the neighbors of each node.\r\n |\r\n |      This object is a read-only dict-like structure with node keys\r\n |      and neighbor-dict values.  The neighbor-dict is keyed by neighbor\r\n"]
[895.292213, "o", " |      to the edge-data-dict.  So `G.adj[3][2]['color'] = 'blue'` sets\r\n |      the color of the edge `(3, 2)` to `\"blue\"`.\r\n |\r\n |      Iterating over G.adj behaves like a dict. Useful idioms include\r\n\u001b[7m--More--\u001b[27m\r |      `for nbr, datadict in G.adj[n].items():`.\r\n"]
[895.292351, "o", " |\r\n"]
[895.292562, "o", " |      The neighbor information is also provided by subscripting the graph.\r\n |      So `for nbr, foovalue in G[node].data('foo', default=1):` works.\r\n |\r\n |      For directed graphs, `G.adj` holds outgoing (successor) info.\r\n |\r\n |  c\bcl\ble\bea\bar\br(self)"]
[895.292637, "o", "\r\n |      Remove all nodes and edges from the graph.\r\n |\r\n |      This also removes the name, and all graph, node, and edge attributes.\r\n |\r\n |      Examples\r\n |      --------\r\n"]
